@model Product

<div class="card card-outline-primary m-1 p-1">
    <div class="bg-faded p-1">
        <h4>
            @Model?.Name
            <span class="badge rounded-pill bg-primary text-white"
                  style="float:right">
                <small>@Model?.Price.ToString("c")</small>
            </span>
        </h4>
    </div>
    <!-- HTML form with ASP.NET Core tag helpers -->
    @* It is an example of Model Binding with forms in ASP.NET.
    Algorithm:
    1. The partial view is rendering when handling pagination, categoryPage, category, default routes.
    During rendering values become filled with HTTPContext and model information we have.
    For example, @ViewContext.HttpContext.Request.PathAndQuery() handles "/" request path and returns it. 
    2. Tag Helper asp-for="ProductId" stands for name="ProductId" + value="@Model.ProductId".
    3. asp-contoller - inbuilt Anchor Tag Helper says to look for route, that handles CartController - shoppingCart route with pattern: "Cart".
    4. By clicking on type submit button, we send HTTP POST request by shoppingCart route. 
    Then [HttpPost] IActionReasult Index(long productId, Uri returnUrl) method takes controll.
    -- Model Binding aspect: when the form is submited, browser collects all input fields and sends them in POST request body in the following format:
    productId=15&returnUrl=/Products/Basketball. 
    Then it Model Binding mechanism compares names of parameters in method and fields in request automatically.
    That's how we transfer data from a web form to the controller. --
*@
    <form id="@Model?.ProductId" asp-controller="Cart" asp-antiforgery="true">
        <input type="hidden" asp-for="ProductId" />
        <input type="hidden" name="returnUrl"
               value="@ViewContext.HttpContext.Request.PathAndQuery()" />
        <span class="card-text p-1">
            @Model?.Description
            <button type="submit"
                    class="btn btn-success btn-sm pull-right" style="float:right">
                Add To Cart
            </button>
        </span>
    </form>
</div>

